Each verb is one method on the item that `Item.open(url)` returns. The tool only opens the item and calls it.

```js
const session = "/framework/ai/sessions/2026/11/06/spawned/mastermind-page-design/";

// get: read a value. Never writes. A computed value (completion, summary) is worked out on the spot.
item({ path: session, get: "tasks.link-dot.state" });   // → "complete"
item({ path: session, get: "completion" });             // → 0.88

// set: change plain fields. Writes one log line.
item({ path: "/live/plan/", set: { title: "Plans" } });

// call: run one method at a dotted path. Writes one log line.
item({ path: session, call: "tasks.add", args: { title: "Plan the item tool" } });
item({ path: session, call: "tasks.link-dot.complete", args: { outcome: "…" } });
```

**Today's way, for comparison:** one `delta` object does all three jobs, and you can tell which job it does only by knowing the class's method names.

```js
item({ path: session, read: "tasks.link-dot.state" });            // get
item({ path: "/live/plan/", delta: { title: "Plans" } });          // set
item({ path: session, delta: { tasks: { add: { title: "…" } } } }); // call
item({ path: session, delta: { completion: {} } });                // a read that looks like a call
```

**The log does not change.** `call: "tasks.add"` still writes `{"tasks":{"add":{…}}}`, the same line as today, so every page and every replay keeps working.

**Your open question, answered** ([core/Session/readme.md](/framework/core/Session/readme.md)): a computed value is a `get`, because a `get` never writes. Keeping a past value is a `call` to a `measure` method, which writes a reading that replay skips:

```js
item({ path: session, call: "measure", args: "completion" });
// writes {"measured":{"completion":0.88}}
```
